fix(gladia): make transcription polling timeout configurable (#24573)

## Background

`transcribe()` with `gladia.transcription()` polls Gladia's asynchronous
pre-recorded job until it reports `done`. The polling loop in
`packages/gladia/src/gladia-transcription-model.ts` had a hard-coded
total budget of 60 seconds (`TranscriptionJobPollingTimedOut`), which is
not configurable. Any job that needs more than a minute — long audio
(Gladia accepts files up to 135 minutes) or jobs with audio intelligence
add-ons such as translation, diarization, summarization, and
audio-to-LLM — failed even though the job finished shortly afterwards.
Neither the timeout nor the poll interval could be adjusted, and the 1
second sleep between polls ignored `abortSignal`.

The gap was found by auditing the `@ai-sdk/gladia` provider against its
OpenAPI spec cached in
[gr2m/ai-provider-monitor](https://github.com/gr2m/ai-provider-monitor)
(`PreRecordedResponse.status` is `queued`/`processing`/`done`/`error`,
and jobs are polled through
`InitPreRecordedTranscriptionResponse.result_url`). A separate issue
(result-schema data loss) is not touched here.

## Summary

- Raise the polling budget default from 60 seconds to 10 minutes and
make it configurable through the new `createGladia({ pollTimeoutMs })`
provider setting, matching the `pollTimeoutMs`/`pollIntervalMs` names
used by the other AI SDK providers.
- Add `createGladia({ pollIntervalMs })` to configure the interval
between status checks (default unchanged at 1 second).
- Allow both settings to be overridden per call with
`providerOptions.gladia.pollIntervalMs` and
`providerOptions.gladia.pollTimeoutMs`. The per-call values are resolved
before the provider settings and are never sent to the Gladia API.
- Validate both settings, at the provider level and in the per-call
options, and reject non-positive, non-integer, or non-finite values with
`InvalidArgumentError`.
- Honor `abortSignal` while polling: the loop stops before the next poll
with `AbortSignal.throwIfAborted()`, and the wait between polls is
cancellable, so a custom abort reason (for example the `TimeoutError`
from `AbortSignal.timeout`) is preserved. A plain abort still rejects
with an `AbortError`-compatible `DOMException`.
- Include the configured timeout in the
`TranscriptionJobPollingTimedOut` message.
- No serialization code was needed for the new settings:
`serializeModelOptions` copies JSON-serializable config keys
automatically, so the workflow round trip already carries them. The test
now does a full `WORKFLOW_SERIALIZE`/`WORKFLOW_DESERIALIZE` round trip
and proves the restored model uses the configured timeout.
- Add deterministic polling, timeout, per-call override, validation,
abort, and workflow-serialization tests, document the provider settings
and provider options in
`content/providers/01-ai-sdk-providers/120-gladia.mdx`, and add a patch
changeset.

## End-to-End Verification

Provider flow exercised: the real Gladia pre-recorded transcription API
through the public `transcribe()` function from `ai` with
`gladia.transcription()`, including the audio upload, job initiation,
and result polling steps.

Both runs used this checkout's locally built packages. The reproduction
lives outside the checkout at
`/tmp/repro-gladia-polling-timeout/repro.mjs` and was executed from
`examples/ai-functions`, whose workspace links resolve `ai` and
`@ai-sdk/gladia` to `packages/*` in this worktree.

Prerequisites (no secrets in this PR):

- `GLADIA_API_KEY` present in `examples/ai-functions/.env`.
- Node.js as required by the repository, dependencies installed with
`command pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`.
- A public-domain LibriVox chapter
(`prideandprejudice_18-19_austen_64kb.mp3`, 47:33, from Internet Archive
item `pride_and_prejudice_librivox`) repeated to ~128 minutes, which is
under Gladia's 8100 second maximum duration.

### Setup: prepare the audio

```bash
mkdir -p /tmp/repro-gladia-polling-timeout
cd /tmp/repro-gladia-polling-timeout
curl -sL -o audio.mp3 \
  "https://archive.org/download/pride_and_prejudice_librivox/prideandprejudice_18-19_austen_64kb.mp3"
python3 - <<'PY'
data = open('audio.mp3', 'rb').read()

def strip(d):
    # drop ID3v2 (with optional footer) and ID3v1 tags so the repeated copies
    # form a single valid frame stream
    if d[:3] == b'ID3':
        size = ((d[6] & 0x7f) << 21) | ((d[7] & 0x7f) << 14) | ((d[8] & 0x7f) << 7) | (d[9] & 0x7f)
        d = d[10 + size + (10 if d[5] & 0x10 else 0):]
    if d[-128:-125] == b'TAG':
        d = d[:-128]
    return d

one = strip(data)
partial = one[: (15_840_960 // 192) * 192]  # ~33 minutes
open('audio-long.mp3', 'wb').write(one + one + partial)  # ~128 minutes
PY
```

### Build and run (from the workspace root / `examples/ai-functions`)

```bash
cd /private/tmp/ai-fix-gladia-polling-timeout
command pnpm exec turbo build --filter=ai... --filter=@ai-sdk/gladia... --output-logs=errors-only

cd examples/ai-functions
node --env-file=/Users/gr2m/code/vercel/ai/examples/ai-functions/.env --input-type=module \
  < /tmp/repro-gladia-polling-timeout/repro.mjs
```

The `before` run loaded the same `GLADIA_API_KEY` from
`examples/ai-functions/.env` into the environment before executing the
identical script.

### Reproduction script (`/tmp/repro-gladia-polling-timeout/repro.mjs`)

```js
// Reproduction: Gladia async transcription polling gives up after a hard-coded
// 60 s total timeout even though the job later reaches `done`.
import { readFile } from 'node:fs/promises';
import { transcribe } from 'ai';
import { gladia } from '@ai-sdk/gladia';

const audio = await readFile('/tmp/repro-gladia-polling-timeout/audio-long.mp3');
console.log(`audio bytes: ${audio.length}`);

const providerOptions = {
  gladia: {
    diarization: true,
    sentences: true,
    subtitles: true,
    namedEntityRecognition: true,
    sentimentAnalysis: true,
    summarization: true,
    summarizationConfig: { type: 'bullet_points' },
    audioToLlm: true,
    audioToLlmConfig: {
      prompts: [
        'Extract key points',
        'List named entities',
        'Describe the tone of the speakers',
      ],
    },
    translation: true,
    translationConfig: {
      model: 'enhanced',
      targetLanguages: [
        'fr', 'de', 'es', 'it', 'pt', 'nl', 'pl', 'ru', 'ja', 'ko',
        'zh', 'ar', 'hi', 'tr', 'sv', 'da', 'fi', 'cs', 'el', 'he',
      ],
    },
  },
};

// Capture the async job URL from the initiate response so we can keep polling
// the job on our own after the SDK gives up.
let resultUrl;
const originalFetch = globalThis.fetch;
globalThis.fetch = async (input, init) => {
  const response = await originalFetch(input, init);
  const url =
    typeof input === 'string'
      ? input
      : input instanceof URL
        ? input.href
        : input.url;
  if (url.endsWith('/v2/pre-recorded')) {
    try {
      const body = await response.clone().json();
      if (body?.result_url) resultUrl = body.result_url;
    } catch {}
  }
  return response;
};

const startedAt = Date.now();
const since = () => `${((Date.now() - startedAt) / 1000).toFixed(1)}s`;
let failure;

try {
  const { text, durationInSeconds } = await transcribe({
    model: gladia.transcription(),
    audio,
    mediaType: 'audio/mpeg',
    providerOptions,
  });
  console.log(`transcribe resolved after ${since()}`);
  console.log(`durationInSeconds: ${durationInSeconds}`);
  console.log(`text[0..80]: ${JSON.stringify(text.slice(0, 80))}`);
} catch (error) {
  failure = error;
  console.log(`transcribe FAILED after ${since()}`);
  console.log(`error name: ${error?.name}`);
  console.log(`error message: ${error?.message}`);
}

if (failure) {
  if (!resultUrl) {
    console.log('no result_url captured; cannot poll the job directly');
    process.exit(0);
  }
  console.log(`polling ${resultUrl} directly to show the job later completes`);
  for (;;) {
    const response = await originalFetch(resultUrl, {
      headers: { 'x-gladia-key': process.env.GLADIA_API_KEY },
    });
    const job = await response.json();
    console.log(`  ${since()} job status: ${job.status}`);
    if (job.status === 'done') {
      console.log(`job reached done ${since()} after transcribe() started`);
      console.log(
        `job audio_duration: ${job.result?.metadata?.audio_duration}s, transcript chars: ${job.result?.transcription?.full_transcript?.length}`,
      );
      break;
    }
    if (job.status === 'error') {
      console.log('job ended with status error');
      break;
    }
    if (Date.now() - startedAt > 15 * 60 * 1000) {
      console.log('gave up polling the job after 15 min');
      break;
    }
    await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 5000));
  }
}
```

### Before the fix

Upload and job initiation take roughly 60 seconds for this file, then
the SDK aborts the poll at its hard-coded 60 second budget, while
polling `result_url` directly shows the job reaching `done` at 169.4 s:

```
audio bytes: 61502784
transcribe FAILED after 122.0s
error name: TranscriptionJobPollingTimedOut
error message: Transcription job polling timed out
polling https://api.gladia.io/v2/pre-recorded/<job-id> directly to show the job later completes
  122.2s job status: processing
  127.9s job status: processing
  133.5s job status: processing
  139.0s job status: processing
  144.6s job status: processing
  150.2s job status: processing
  155.7s job status: processing
  169.4s job status: done
job reached done 169.4s after transcribe() started
job audio_duration: 7687.808s, transcript chars: 104698
```

### After the fix

The same script, unchanged and still using the default `gladia` provider
instance, now waits for the job and returns the transcript. This run was
done after the review follow-up commit (`pollIntervalMs`/`pollTimeoutMs`
plus per-call overrides):

```
audio bytes: 61502784
transcribe resolved after 216.6s
durationInSeconds: 7687.808
text[0..80]: "Till Elizabeth entered the drawing-room at Netherfield, and looked in vain for M"
```

Verification limits: the reproduction uses a 128 minute public-domain
recording with audio intelligence add-ons; job timing on Gladia varies
with queue load, so the observed durations differ between runs (122 s /
169 s before, 175.4 s in the earlier after-run, 216.6 s in the
post-review after-run). Multi-hour files beyond Gladia's 8100 second
maximum were rejected by the API (HTTP 400), so the reproduction stays
below that limit. The `abortSignal` cancellation and the per-call
overrides are covered by unit tests rather than an additional live run.

